1. The bin layout

Every standard command is its own real ELF32 binary: the sources live in
bin/ (cat.c, ls.c, cp.c, vi.c, ...) and the Makefile compiles each one
with plain host gcc -m32 (freestanding, no libc) into a standalone ELF32
binary in home/bin/ (<name>.o), embedded in the boot image (via home.tar)
as /bin/<name>.o. The kernel loads them at runtime through its in-kernel
ELF32 driver (kernel/elf.c), which resolves each program's symbols against
the kernel export table (include/tanja.h).
The shell runs a command by trying /bin/<name>.o first, then
/usr/bin/<name>.o (packages installed by pkg), then /Programs/<name>(.o).
Doom and TinyCC are not embedded in /bin by default; install them from the
package repository with `sudo pkg install doom` or `sudo pkg install tcc`.

2. Programming in TanjaOS

TinyCC is installed from the package repository as /usr/bin/tcc.o, so 'tcc' is just another command. It compiles
real C99/C11 (the full tcc frontend) into ELF32 objects the kernel ELF
driver can run. The OS ships a real, standard C header set at /usr/include
- stdio.h, stdlib.h, string.h, unistd.h, ctype.h, time.h, math.h, errno.h,
assert.h, setjmp.h, signal.h, fcntl.h, inttypes.h, limits.h, locale.h,
fenv.h, iso646.h, stdatomic.h, stdalign.h, stdnoreturn.h, tgmath.h,
sys/... - and NOTHING custom: no tanja.h. The kernel API (console, printf,
keyboard, the fs_* filesystem functions, environment, timers) is declared
inside those standard headers, so a TanjaOS program is just a normal C
file.

tcc is strict by default, on purpose:

  * ALL warnings are hard compile-time errors (-Werror is injected
    first in the argument list).  Pass -Wno-error to get normal
    warning behavior back.  (#warning directives are exempt, like
    a real gcc -Werror build.)
  * After a clean compile the wrapper verifies EVERY external symbol
    the produced object references against the live kernel export
    table (tanja_export_count()/tanja_export_name() in kernel/elf.c).
    A program that calls something the kernel does not export fails
    AT COMPILE TIME with "undefined symbol" instead of failing to
    load at run time.  Weak undefined symbols are exempt (they bind
    to 0, per the C standard).
  * C11 u8"..." string literals work (parsed as UTF-8 narrow
    strings).  u/U wide literals are a clear compile-time error
    (there is no wchar/char16_t machinery).

    #include <stdio.h>

    void main(char* args) {
        printf("hi %s\n", args);
        return 0;
    }

Edit it with 'vi hello.c', compile and run it:

    tcc -c hello.c -o hello.o      # compile
    ./hello.o some args            # run

There is no linker, so 'tcc hello.c -o hello' (link an executable)
is not supported; the wrapper downgrades it to compile-only, names
the result hello.o and prints how to run it.

Logins start in the user's home directory, /home/home (shown as ~ in
the prompt; bare `cd` goes there too).

To run any program, just type its name: './hello.o', 'hello.o' or
'dir/prog.o' all work (there is no exec command - if a token isn't a
builtin or a /bin command but names an existing file, the shell runs
it: ELF32 objects as programs, text files as line-per-command scripts).

Programs are entered as void main(char* args) (the single command-line
argument string) - int main(void) works too. The kernel exports the full
stdio/stdlib/string/ctype/time/math surface (printf/scanf families,
malloc/calloc/realloc/free, FILE streams incl. fopen/fread/fwrite/fclose,
open/close/read/write/lseek, stat/fstat, qsort/bsearch, strtol family,
the x87 math library in all three tiers, locale/fenv/iso646 support,
64-bit division/modulo helpers __divdi3 and friends), so ordinary C
programs compile and run as-is. Anything NOT exported is caught at
compile time by the wrapper's symbol check (see above).

3. Building

Use 'make' to compile the kernel and standard bin/ commands. TinyCC source/build
files remain under tools/tcc, and Doom source remains under ports/doom; neither
is embedded as /bin/tcc.o or /bin/doom.o by default. Once compiling is finished, a bootable OS
image 'tanja-base' will be located at arch/x86/boot, boot it with:

    qemu-system-i386 -m 64 -kernel arch/x86/boot/tanja-base
 ___________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES ]
|-------------------|
| 1) make           |
| 2) nasm           |
| 3) gcc            |
|___________________|

For compiling on BSD systems, you should use gmake instead of make,
this is because BSD's make can't use the traditional linker.ld for TanjaOS.
You will see a file called "Makefile.bsd", this is the Makefile for BSD systems.
To use Makefile.bsd, use 'gmake <option> -f Makefile.bsd'.
 _________________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES (BSD) ]
|-------------------------|
| 1) gmake                |
| 2) nasm                 |
| 3) gcc                  |
|_________________________|

4. Networking, Wi-Fi and pkg

Ethernet: kernel/nic.c drives Intel e1000/e1000e (PRO/1000) and Realtek
RTL8139 NICs; kernel/net.c is the IPv4 stack (ARP, ICMP, UDP, DHCP, DNS,
TCP client, plain-HTTP GET). The adapter is configured automatically at
boot via DHCP (and again when a cable is plugged in later).

    ifconfig            show address / link        ifconfig dhcp   re-run DHCP
    ping <host|ip>

Wi-Fi: 'wifi' opens a menu (arrows scroll long lists, Enter connect, h hidden
network, r rescan, d disconnect, f forget, a next adapter, q quit). Also
scriptable: wifi list | status | saved | connect <ssid> [password] |
disconnect | forget <ssid> | adapter [N]. kernel/wifi.c is the framework radio
drivers plug into: up to 4 adapters, open/WEP/WPA2/WPA3/802.1X reporting,
password validation, scan lists sorted + de-duplicated, saved networks in
/etc/wifi.conf (root-only) with autoconnect to the strongest saved one.
kernel/wpa.c is the chip-independent WPA2-PSK core (passphrase->key, 4-way and
group handshakes, key unwrap, AES-CCM). At boot and in 'wifi' / 'wifi status' it reports any Wi-Fi card it finds on the PCI
bus (vendor:device ids) even without a driver. There is no real radio driver yet. 'make WIFI_DEMO=1' adds two fake adapters
(password for secured demo networks: demo1234) for trying all of this out.

pkg (root required; use sudo from a user account):
    sudo pkg install <name>   GET <repo>/<name> and install the binary to /usr/bin/<name>.o
    sudo pkg remove <name>    delete /usr/bin/<name>.o (only for packages pkg installed)

Set it with 'export PKG_REPO=http://host:port' or /etc/pkg.repo. Default source: https://github.com/MSK-Kernel/pkg_<name>/raw/main/<name>
(fetched directly over https). With a PKG_REPO or /etc/pkg.repo override
it fetches <repo>/<name> (no extension) instead, http or https.
An install marker is kept in /var/pkg/<name> so pkg can tell its packages
from system commands. The shell searches /bin, then /usr/bin, then /Programs.

https: kernel/tls.c wraps BearSSL (tools/bearssl, MIT licence, see its
LICENSE.txt) - TLS 1.2, certificate chain + host name verified against the
Mozilla roots in kernel/tls_anchors.h (generated from certifi with brssl;
regenerate it to refresh the roots). No TLS 1.3. The clock is the CMOS RTC
(never earlier than the build date); entropy is RDRAND + timing jitter, so
it is weaker on CPUs without RDRAND.
Try it locally: 'python3 -m http.server 8000' in a dir of source files on the host.

5. OpenBSD Wi-Fi code (bsd/)

For broad Wi-Fi chip support TanjaOS reuses OpenBSD's drivers instead of writing its own.
bsd/src holds OpenBSD's 802.11 stack (net80211), its Intel Wireless-AC driver (if_iwm.c:
3160/3165/3168/7260/7265/8260/8265/9260/9560) and the crypto helpers they need, unmodified
(licences are in each file). bsd/include has the OpenBSD kernel headers they include.
bsd/compat is TanjaOS's own layer: the ~110 OpenBSD kernel services those files call
(memory, mbufs, timers/tasks, sleeping, PCI, DMA, network interface), polled from the idle
loop. 'wifi selftest' runs 50+ checks of that layer. Status: layer + build done and tested;
the driver is in the kernel but is NOT started at boot. To try it on an Intel Wireless-AC card
(experimental, untested on real hardware): copy the card's iwlwifi-*.ucode firmware into
home/lib/firmware/ (see the README there), run make, boot, then run 'wifi attach' and read
the iwm0 messages it prints. Only the Intel AC family (iwm) is attached; AX200/AX201 (iwx),
Atheros, Realtek and Broadcom are not.

6. Testing the Wi-Fi driver on a PC that can't boot TanjaOS (UEFI only, no Legacy BIOS/CSM)

Run TanjaOS in QEMU on that PC and pass the real Wi-Fi card through to it (VFIO):
    sudo apt install qemu-system-x86
    make                          # copies the Intel firmware from /lib/firmware automatically
    tools/vfio-wifi.sh check      # IOMMU on? what else is in the card's IOMMU group?
    sudo tools/vfio-wifi.sh bind  # Debian loses that Wi-Fi card until 'unbind' or a reboot
    sudo tools/vfio-wifi.sh run   # TanjaOS opens in QEMU; its output is also printed in this terminal
    (inside TanjaOS)  wifi attach     then     wifi
    sudo tools/vfio-wifi.sh unbind
Everything TanjaOS prints is mirrored to COM1, so the terminal log can be copied as plain text.
If 'check' says the IOMMU is off: enable VT-d (AMD-Vi) in the BIOS and add intel_iommu=on iommu=pt
(amd_iommu=on) to the kernel command line. If the card shares its IOMMU group with other devices
the passthrough may be refused. Untested on real hardware.
